\frametitle{Separation \emph {is} independence --- formally}
The separating conjunction splits a probability space into an
  \alert{independent product} \(\sigma_1 \star \sigma_2\):
  \begin{leancode}
sep P Q := ⟨fun σ ↦ ∃ σ₁ σ₂, ∃ (σ₁₂ : ✓'(σ₁ ⋆ σ₂)),
                     ↓σ₁₂ ≤ σ ∧ P.1 σ₁ ∧ Q.1 σ₂, …⟩
  \end{leancode}
  \pause
  This is not just a slogan --- it is a theorem. Owning two random
  variables separately entails that they are genuinely independent:
  \begin{leancode}
lemma indep_of_own {X Y : RV ⟪Ty.real⟫} (Ω : PSp)
    (ownXY : (iprop(own X ∗ own Y) : LProp).1 Ω) :
    IndepFun (μ := Ω.μ) X Y
  \end{leancode}
  \pause
  \begin{center}
    \small
    \(\bigl(\text{own } X * \text{own } Y\bigr)
      \ \Longrightarrow\
      X \perp\!\!\!\perp Y\)
    \qquad with the magic wand \(\wand\) available for hypothetical reasoning.
  \end{center}
